<title>USA: Md. emissions test to be mandatory to save funds.</title>
<headline>Md. emissions test to be mandatory to save funds.</headline>
<dateline>ANNAPOLIS, Md. 1997-05-20</dateline>
<text>
<p>Maryland's unpopular auto emissions test that requires cars run on a treadmill is being made mandatory, in order to save about $90 million of federal aid contingent on the test.</p>
<p>Gov. Parris Glendening vetoed a measure passed by the legislature earlier this year that would have kept the program voluntary.</p>
<p>Federal officials have been pressuring the state to make the test mandatory, warning that highway construction funds could otherwise be withheld.</p>
<p>Glendening said everyone will be required to run the test starting October 1.</p>
<p>--U.S. Municipal Desk, 212-859-1650</p>
</text>
